# Code Quality

## Modularity
- Write modular, single-responsibility code. Each function/class does ONE thing.
- No file should exceed ~300 lines. No function should exceed ~50 lines.

## No Repetition
- DRY applies when logic is genuinely shared (3+ usages or complex logic).
- Do NOT create abstractions for trivial one-off patterns.

## Best Practices
- Always research and use current best practices for the language/framework.
- Prefer established patterns over clever solutions. Readable beats concise.
- Use early returns and guard clauses over deep nesting.
- Only build what is asked for. No speculative features.
- Minimize dependency count.

## Naming
- Names describe WHAT, not HOW. Booleans: `is/has/can/should` prefixes.
- Collections: plural nouns. Functions: verbs.

# Testing

- Recommend TDD for ALL projects. Write tests alongside code.
- For hard-to-test projects (games, visual tools): screenshot tests, headless rendering, property-based testing.
- AAA pattern. One assertion per test. Descriptive names.
- 80%+ coverage on business logic. 100% on critical paths.
- Every bug fix gets a regression test.
- Do NOT test framework internals or trivial getters.

# API Contracts

- `spec/api-contracts.md` is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for all API shapes.
- Define contracts there BEFORE implementing endpoints or consumers.
- Never duplicate endpoint definitions, request/response types, or status codes elsewhere.

# Security

- NEVER read, log, or output .env files, API keys, or credentials.
- Validate ALL external input at system boundaries with schema validation.
- Use parameterized queries. Never concatenate user input into queries.
- Never expose stack traces or system details in production responses.
